WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:13.860
Jules was absolutely delightful, and it grieves me so much, not just that he passed, but that people like you never got to meet him.

2
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:21.720
You want to get an idea of his personality, Google “Cajun Night Before” . . . not Google, YouTube “Cajun Night Before Christmas.”

3
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.140
He was like that all the time.

4
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:31.400
He was always funny, inspiring, he could be serious,

5
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:37.260
and when he was serious it was like that uncle you went to when you had a problem, you know.

6
00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.780
But he was a joy to work with.

7
00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.220
And he was very hands off.

8
00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:44.980
He didn’t micromanage the station at all.

9
00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:49.580
All he wanted was to make sure that you followed the FCC rules,

10
00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:54.360
and he kept the station turned on in his office, you know, to listen to see.

11
00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:02.420
And if somebody needed a little guidance, like, “Honey, listen to me carefully,

12
00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:10.620
there’s no such thing as ‘the weather outside,’ we better hope the weather’s inside.” [laughs] That kind of thing.

13
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:16.000
He talked about economy of language and things like that if you were a deejay.

14
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:23.000
And, you know, he’d help people who were new with learning to speak efficiently.

15
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:32.980
Back then, we actually had to know our music well enough so we’d know that it was like eight beats until the singing started,

16
00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:42.560
so when you hit the . . . when you hit that pot and turned that on, you were talking right up until the singing started.

17
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:50.000
You didn’t have it all computerized now where you know you have exactly seventeen seconds to say what you need to say, you had to remember.

18
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.280
He taught you how to pace yourself.

19
00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.940
He taught you how to be articulate.

20
00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:56.920
He was so patient.

21
00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.360
He was so fun.

22
00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:59.860
He was a fabulous teacher.

23
00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:06.780
I really consider myself tremendously fortunate to have had him in my life.

24
00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:12.260
And he treated all of his students like adults, because we were adults.

25
00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.380
He said, “You’re not kids, you’re adults!”

26
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:20.380
If you were lucky and he had a barbecue, he invited the station over to his house.

27
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:21.180
“I’m having a barbecue Sunday.

28
00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:25.060
Everybody who’s not on the air, come on over.”

29
00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:25.920
He was amazing.
